 ## Overview
-Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) refers to a per-scene optimization process that refines an initial Gaussian representation to achieve high-fidelity reconstruction or adaptation to specific constraints. It addresses the problem of initializing a scene representation from sparse or noisy data (e.g., generated point clouds, low-resolution inputs, or stylized images) by treating the reconstruction as a differentiable optimization problem supervised by rendered images [S1, S3][S4, unofficial]. Unlike single-stage training from scratch, fine-tuning leverages pretrained priors or initial estimates to accelerate convergence and improve geometric consistency [S3]. It sets itself apart from general densification strategies by focusing on the refinement of existing attributes (geometry, color, opacity) rather than solely managing primitive count, though it often enables growing and pruning during the process [S3].
